<%# The rating scale, as a fixed footer the session scrolls behind. #277, and it is called
    "RPE Advice" because that is what the issue asks it be called -- "How do I rate this?"
    was a question, and a question sitting permanently on screen reads as one being put to
    you rather than as a reference you may open.

    It was rendered once per lift panel until now, which is the arrangement this replaces
    and which was not wrong when it was written: the scale belongs near the buttons it
    explains, and one copy at the foot of a long page was a scroll away from all of them.
    Panels made that worse rather than better -- the copy was then a swipe *and* a scroll
    away -- so a copy per panel was how it stayed close.

    Pinning it removes the reason for the duplication entirely. A footer is never further
    away than the bottom of the screen, from any panel, at any scroll position, so one copy
    is now closer than five were. That is the trade #277 is asking for, and it also ends
    two small costs the repetition carried: five open tables were reachable at once, and
    five copies of a table meant the same 2 KB of markup five times over.

    Fixed rather than sticky. Sticky positions inside the flow, so it would have to sit at
    the foot of a scroller -- and the one on this screen is horizontal, scroll-snapped, and
    swapped out by htmx on every tap. Fixed takes it out of the flow entirely, which is
    what lets the content scroll behind it and what keeps it out of the panels' way.

    Rendered from views/workouts/session.erb rather than from the body, and that placement
    is load-bearing: #session-body and the panels inside it are replaced by htmx on every
    Done tap and every fifteen-second poll, and a <details> the lifter had opened would
    snap shut each time. Outside the swapped region it holds its own open state with no
    JavaScript and nothing to re-bind.

    bg-gray-100 is the page's own background rather than transparency, so the rows passing
    underneath are covered rather than showing through the table. The border is what says
    the footer is a different surface from the session above it.

    max-h and overflow-y-auto because this table is taller than a phone: opened without
    them it would cover the whole screen, including the Done buttons it exists to explain.
    svh rather than vh is the unit that accounts for iOS Safari's URL bar -- vh is the
    *large* viewport, measured as though the bar were collapsed, so 70vh of an expanded
    viewport is taller than what is actually visible.

    env(safe-area-inset-bottom) keeps the summary above the home indicator on a notched
    phone. Zero everywhere else, so it costs nothing on a device without one.

    The chevron is not decoration. A summary is display:list-item and draws a triangle by
    default, but this one is a flex row so the marker never rendered -- the label was a line
    of bold text with nothing to say it opened. It rotates on open through group-open, which
    is CSS, so it works with JavaScript off like the disclosure itself.

    Closed by default, which matters more now than it did: this one is on screen at all
    times, and a table that opens itself would be covering the session every time the page
    loads. %>
<details class="group fixed inset-x-0 bottom-0 z-20 border-t border-gray-300 bg-gray-100
                pb-[env(safe-area-inset-bottom)]">
  <summary class="mx-auto flex min-h-11 max-w-md cursor-pointer items-center px-4 text-sm
                  font-medium text-gray-700"><%== render('_chevron') %>RPE Advice</summary>
  <div class="mx-auto max-h-[70svh] max-w-md space-y-4 overflow-y-auto px-4 pb-4 text-sm text-gray-600">
    <p>The most reliable observable is bar speed, because it degrades before your perception of effort does.</p>
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left align-top">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-gray-300 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">
          <th scope="col" class="py-1 pr-3 font-medium">RPE</th>
          <th scope="col" class="py-1 pr-3 font-medium">Reps left</th>
          <th scope="col" class="py-1 font-medium">What you&rsquo;d notice</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 align-top">
        <tr>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">6</td>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3">4+</td>
          <td class="py-1">Every rep the same speed. Feels like warmup that got heavy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">7</td>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3">3</td>
          <td class="py-1">Still fast. You&rsquo;d bet money on 3 more.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">8</td>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3">2</td>
          <td class="py-1">Last rep visibly slower than the first. Sure about 2, not 3.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">9</td>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3">1</td>
          <td class="py-1">One rep clearly slowed. You know there&rsquo;s one more, and only one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">10</td>
          <td class="py-1 pr-3">0</td>
          <td class="py-1">The last rep ground. Nothing left.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div>
      <p class="font-medium text-gray-700">Four checks that beat guessing</p>
      <ul class="mt-1 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li><span class="font-medium text-gray-700">Rate it before you rack it.</span> The number degrades within seconds of setting the bar down &mdash; relief rewrites the memory of effort.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-gray-700">Ask it as a bet.</span> &ldquo;Would I put money on two more?&rdquo; A confident yes is 8 or lower. Hesitating is a 9.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-gray-700">Film a set.</span> A phone propped against your bag is enough. Watching the first rep against the last makes speed loss obvious in a way it never is from under the bar.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-gray-700">Form drift counts.</span> Hips shooting on a squat, elbows flaring on a bench &mdash; that&rsquo;s the body borrowing leverage, and it means 9+ regardless of how it felt.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p>One calibration exercise worth doing once: take a set you&rsquo;d call RPE 8 and keep going to true failure. If you got four more reps, your 8 is really a 6, and every load in the program is too light. Most people undershoot by one to two points at first. Coming off a layoff you&rsquo;ll likely overshoot instead &mdash; detrained lifts feel harder than they are.</p>
  </div>
</details>
